        <section class="faq-section">
            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Can I use a trigger sprayer for a flammable formulation?</h4>
                <p>Yes, but the sprayer and bottle must be made of materials that are chemically compatible and don't build up static. However, for highly flammable large-scale applications, aerosols with flame-retardant propellants or specialized safety valves are often preferred for controlled delivery.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Why is my pump spray "spitting" instead of misting?</h4>
                <p>Spitting is usually caused by either a formulation that is too viscous for the pump's orifice size or air being trapped in the pump chamber. It can also occur if the user doesn't press the pump firmly and quickly, as manual pumps rely on velocity to induce atomization.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="faq-item">
                <h4>Are aerosol cans recyclable?</h4>
                <p>Yes, aluminum and tinplate aerosol cans are 100% recyclable. However, they must be completely empty before disposal. Some municipalities require them to be punctured using specialized tools, while others accept them in standard recycling streams once the pressure is relieved.</p>
            </div>
